<h1>System reference</h1>
<p>Most installations do not need anything on this page. Use it when preparing managed, offline,
  minimal or hardware-connected machines, or when diagnosing an environment-specific issue.</p>

<h2 id="operating-systems">Operating systems</h2>
<p>Orcraft supports Windows, Linux and macOS. It has been validated in these environments:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>Version</th>
      <th>Kernel / build</th>
      <th>Architecture</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>11</td>
      <td>build 26200</td>
      <td>x86_64</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>10</td>
      <td>build 19045</td>
      <td>x86_64</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Ubuntu</td>
      <td>24.04</td>
      <td>kernel 6.17 and 7.0</td>
      <td>x86_64</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS</td>
      <td>26.2</td>
      <td>Darwin 25.2.0</td>
      <td>arm64</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Other versions and architectures - arm64 Linux, Windows on Arm, Intel Macs - are expected to
  work wherever Python and PySide6 are available; they simply have not been through the validation
  matrix above yet.</p>
<p>Windows 10 left mainstream support in October 2025, but it stays in the matrix on purpose: lab
  and instrument machines often cannot be upgraded, and endpoints run on it perfectly well.</p>
<p>The controller GUI's best experience is on Windows. It needs a desktop session to run.
  Endpoints have no GUI, so they can run headless - on a server or over SSH.</p>

<h3 id="linux-desktop-libraries" class="req req--if">Linux desktop libraries (controller only)</h3>
<p>Desktop Linux installations already include the Qt libraries used by the controller. Use these
  commands only on a minimal image where startup fails with
  <code>Could not load the Qt platform plugin "xcb"</code>. Endpoints have no GUI and need none of
  these packages.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Distribution</th>
      <th>Command</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Debian, Ubuntu</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo apt install libgl1 libxkbcommon-x11-0 libxcb-cursor0") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Fedora, RHEL</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo dnf install mesa-libGL libxkbcommon-x11 xcb-util-cursor") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="endpoint-connectivity">Endpoint connectivity</h2>
<p>What Orcraft opens on the network and how the controller finds an endpoint. Use it to plan
  firewall rules, choose ports and addresses on machines with several interfaces, and decide whether
  endpoints are added by name or by IP address. The endpoint's <code>-port</code> and
  <code>-ip</code> options are given on its command line, in the same way as on
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/#run-endpoint">Adding endpoints/Run the endpoint</a>.
  The subsections that follow the table cover host-name resolution - where a name is resolved, hosts
  with more than one address, stale lookups and IPv6 link-local answers - and running several
  endpoint applications on one machine.</p>
<h3 id="ports-addresses-and-names">Ports, addresses and names</h3>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Endpoint port</th>
      <td>TCP 50051 by default; configurable with <code>-port</code>. The controller must be given
        the same port when the endpoint is added</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Endpoint applications per machine</th>
      <td>A controller registers each machine once. A second copy of the endpoint application on
        another port lets another controller use the same machine - see
        <a class="text-accent" href="#several-endpoints-on-one-machine">Several endpoint
        applications on one machine</a> below</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Address families</th>
      <td>The endpoint listens dual-stack, IPv4 and IPv6; restrict it to one address with
        <code>-ip</code> (<code>-bind</code> is an alias). The controller's reachability ping is
        IPv4-only on Windows and Linux, so an endpoint reachable only over IPv6 shows as Offline from
        those controllers</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Controller</th>
      <td>Outbound connections only; no inbound controller rule</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Discovery</th>
      <td>No broadcast, multicast or discovery ports of its own. Host-name lookups go through the
        operating system's resolver, including its mDNS (<code>.local</code>) fallback</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Host names</th>
      <td>Optional - endpoints can be added by IP address. A DNS record or local
        <code>hosts</code> entry works when names are preferred; a name absent from DNS is also tried
        as <code>name.local</code> - see
        <a class="text-accent" href="#hosts-with-several-addresses">Hosts with more than one address</a> and
        <a class="text-accent" href="#stale-name-lookups">Stale name lookups</a> below</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<h3 id="connectivity-commands">Commands</h3>
<p>Commands that follow from the table:</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Show every option</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("orcraft-endpoint") }} - run without a subcommand, the endpoint prints its help
        and exits</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Listen on another port</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("orcraft-endpoint run -port 50052") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Listen on one address only</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("orcraft-endpoint run -ip 10.100.102.73") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Name without a DNS record (Windows)</th>
      <td>Add a line to {{ cmd.copyCell("C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Name without a DNS record (macOS, Linux)</th>
      <td>Add a line to {{ cmd.copyCell("/etc/hosts") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>For the firewall steps needed by remote endpoints, see
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/prerequisites/#remote-endpoints">Prerequisites/Remote endpoints</a>.</p>

<h3 id="host-name-resolution">Host-name resolution</h3>
<p>An endpoint added by host name is resolved on the machine that runs the controller, by that
  machine's own resolver: its DNS server, its <code>hosts</code> file and, on most systems, mDNS
  (<code>name.local</code>) as a fallback. The controller pings the name, then <code>name.local</code>,
  then the last address it knew. A name that works from one PC can therefore fail from another, and
  the place to check is always the controller's machine. Ping the name there; the first line of the
  output shows which address the name resolved to:</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Windows</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("ping -4 ENDPOINT-HOST") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>macOS</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("ping -c 1 ENDPOINT-HOST") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Linux</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("ping -4 -c 1 ENDPOINT-HOST") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="hosts-with-several-addresses">Hosts with more than one address</h3>
<p>A machine connected by both Ethernet and Wi-Fi has two IPv4 addresses, and a name lookup can
  return either of them. The controller and the endpoint talk over whichever address answers, but the
  address shown on the card is the one the controller's last lookup returned, so it can differ from one
  lookup to the next. When one of the interfaces is disconnected, the name can keep resolving to that
  interface's address until the cached answer expires, and the card shows Offline while the machine is
  reachable on its other address. To hold a host to one address, add the endpoint by IP address instead
  of by name, or keep only one of its interfaces on the network the controller uses.</p>

<h3 id="stale-name-lookups">Stale name lookups</h3>
<p>Resolvers cache their answers. After an endpoint machine changes address, disconnects an
  interface or is renamed, the controller's machine can keep pinging the old address for minutes.
  Flush the cache on the controller's machine, then wait for the card's next poll:</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Windows</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("ipconfig /flushdns") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>macOS</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo dscacheutil -flushcache; sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Linux (systemd-resolved)</th>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("resolvectl flush-caches") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>On Windows, ping counts a "Destination host unreachable" reply from the controller's own machine
  as an answer, so a stale address can show the card as
  <strong class="text-warning">Online</strong> rather than Offline. The flush applies in the same way.</p>

<h3 id="ipv6-link-local-answers">IPv6 link-local answers</h3>
<p>Some resolvers return an IPv6 link-local address (<code>fe80::…</code>) ahead of the IPv4 one;
  a plain ping of the name shows it in the first line. Such an address is tied to one network interface
  of the controller's machine and stops working when that machine moves between Wi-Fi and Ethernet.
  If connections to an endpoint are unstable while its name resolves that way, add the endpoint by its
  IPv4 address.</p>


<h3 id="several-endpoints-on-one-machine">Several endpoint applications on one machine</h3>
<p>The endpoint application is the program started with <code>orcraft-endpoint run</code>. One
  running copy listens on one port. A machine normally runs one copy, and the controller registers
  that machine as one endpoint.</p>
<h4 id="each-machine-is-added-once">Each machine is added once</h4>
<p>A controller registers a machine as one endpoint. If the machine is already in the Endpoints
  frame, adding it again is refused, even with a different port.</p>
<p>Several tests can still use the same machine. By default, runs wait for each other on that
  endpoint. If tests may run on the machine at the same time, set the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#endpoint-options"><code>shared</code></a>
  option on the endpoint field in the test.</p>
<h4 id="a-second-endpoint-application-for-another-controller">A second endpoint application for another controller</h4>
<p>A machine can run a second copy of the endpoint application on another port, so that another
  controller can use the machine. Each controller then talks to its own endpoint application. The
  two controllers work independently, and packages uploaded through one endpoint application do not
  affect the other.</p>
<p>Start the second copy with a port of its own, and register it in the second controller with that
  port:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("orcraft-endpoint run -port 50052") }}
<p>Use this only when the tests of the two controllers do not compete for the same resource on the
  machine, such as a device, a port or a file. If they do, register the same endpoint application in
  both controllers instead. Runs from both controllers then wait for each other, or run at the same
  time when the test marks the endpoint <code>shared</code>.</p>
<p>The endpoint application reports only its host name to the controller. The Info dialog of an
  endpoint shows its <strong class="text-accent">Port</strong>, which tells the two copies apart.</p>
<h4 id="requirements-for-a-second-instance">Requirements for a second endpoint application</h4>
<p>The endpoint application keeps its files under the directory it was started from and in the
  Python environment it was installed in. A second copy therefore needs the following:</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Its own port</th>
      <td>A port that is already in use is refused at startup</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Its own launch directory</th>
      <td>The endpoint application writes its <code>Logs</code> and unpacks received packages into
        <code>orcraft-packages</code> under the directory it was started from. Start each copy from
        a different directory. Otherwise both use the same package folder, and one copy can change
        what the other one runs</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Its own installation, to update independently</th>
      <td>An update from the controller reinstalls the endpoint application into the Python
        environment it runs from, and restarts only that copy. If two copies run from the same
        installation, both receive the new version, but the second one keeps running the old code
        until it is restarted. Install each copy in its own
        <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/prerequisites/#virtual-environment">virtual environment</a>
        to update them independently</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Its own start</th>
      <td><code>orcraft-endpoint startup</code> registers one auto-start entry. Running it again for
        a second port replaces the first entry. Start the second copy by hand or with your own
        scheduler</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>A machine other than the controller's</th>
      <td>The controller refuses to add <code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code> or
        <code>::1</code>, because it already has a built-in local endpoint</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<h4 id="security-material">Security material</h4>
<p>The authorization token and the certificates are stored once per machine. All copies of the
  endpoint application on the machine use the same ones.</p>

<h2 id="permissions">Permissions and storage</h2>
<p>Orcraft itself does not require administrator or root access.</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th>Controller and endpoint</th>
      <td>Run as the logged-in user - no elevation or system service required</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Profile data</th>
      <td>Security material and downloaded wheels use <code>%APPDATA%\Orcraft</code>, or
        <code>~/.orcraft</code> elsewhere</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Working directory</th>
      <td>The controller writes <code>Logs</code>, <code>Sessions</code> and <code>Reports</code>
        where it was launched; endpoints unpack received packages there too. Launch from a writable
        directory. <code>ORCRAFT_DATA_ROOT</code> redirects controller output</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Listening port</th>
      <td>Non-privileged by default; any port above 1023 needs no elevation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th>Test steps</th>
      <td>Inherit the endpoint user's rights. Hardware tests may need additional device permissions
        or an elevated endpoint session</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="shells-and-terminals">Shells and terminals</h2>

<h3 id="command-shell">Command shell</h3>
<p>Every supported OS already includes the shell Orcraft uses. CLI commands in test steps run in:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Endpoint OS</th>
      <th>Commands run in</th>
      <th>Not</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td><code>cmd.exe</code></td>
      <td>PowerShell</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td><code>/bin/sh</code></td>
      <td>bash or zsh, so their syntax is not available by default</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>If a step needs a particular shell, request it in the command:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('powershell -NoProfile -Command "Get-Process orcraft-endpoint"
bash -c "for i in {1..3}; do ping -c 1 10.0.0.5; done"', 'bash') }}

<h3 id="terminal-output">Terminal output</h3>
<p>The endpoint and test framework use ANSI colors and Unicode box-drawing characters.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Terminal</th>
      <th>Colored output</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows Terminal, PowerShell 7</td>
      <td>Works as-is</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows legacy console (conhost)</td>
      <td>May print raw codes such as <code>&#8592;[92m</code> - see below</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS Terminal.app, iTerm2</td>
      <td>Works as-is</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux emulators - gnome-terminal, konsole, xfce4-terminal, kitty, alacritty, xterm</td>
      <td>Works as-is</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>If a Windows console shows raw escape sequences, use Windows Terminal or enable virtual-terminal
  processing once for the current user:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("reg add HKCU\\Console /v VirtualTerminalLevel /t REG_DWORD /d 1") }}
<p>If box-drawing characters appear as <code>?</code>, use Windows Terminal or run
  <code>chcp 65001</code> in the classic console. The controller GUI renders output itself and is
  unaffected.</p>

<h3 id="log-files">Log files</h3>
<p>Runs are saved as <code>.ansi</code> files with their colors embedded.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Reading a saved log</th>
      <th>How</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Controller</td>
      <td>Renders logs directly in its GUI and opens saved <code>.ansi</code> files</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("cat") }} or {{ cmd.copyCell("less -R") }} to scroll with colors intact</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("Get-Content") }} in Windows Terminal</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Text editors</td>
      <td>Show the escape codes unless the editor understands ANSI</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="python-libraries">Python libraries</h2>
<p>pip installs these libraries automatically along with the <code>orcraft</code> package.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Library</th>
      <th>Used for</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>PySide6</td>
      <td>The controller's GUI</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
